[#:seon.task{:name "a-surface-id-passes-only-ascii-letters-digits-dot-and-hyphen"
             :intent "`seon.render.block/surface-id` passes every Unicode letter or digit through unescaped, against its docstring: only `[A-Za-z0-9.-]` pass through, `_` doubles and every other character becomes `_<hex>_`. Its body tests each character with Character/isLetterOrDigit, which is true for `\u00e9`, `\u00aa` and the Arabic-Indic digit `\u0663`, so `(seon.render.block/surface-id (keyword \"\u00e9\"))` answers \"surface-\u00e9\" where its docstring promises \"surface-_e9_\". A character passes through only when it is an ASCII letter (a-z, A-Z), an ASCII digit (0-9), `.` or `-`; the escape and the injectivity that `seon.render.block-test/stable-surface-addresses-are-injective` proves stay as they are. Its contract is already complete: input `:seon.render.block/name` (a keyword), output `:seon.render/surface-id` (a string of at least 9 characters); the property generates names with `clojure.test.check.generators/string` under `clojure.test.check.generators/such-that` `clojure.core/seq` (an empty name is a separate defect, not this task) and checks them with `clojure.test.check/quick-check`. Read `seon.render.block/surface-id` and `seon.render.block-test/stable-surface-addresses-are-injective`."
             :seon.task/children #{[:seon.task/sym seon.render.block/prove-a-surface-id-passes-only-ascii]
                                   [:seon.task/sym seon.render.block/implement-a-surface-id-passes-only-ascii]}}
 #:seon.task{:name "prove-a-surface-id-passes-only-ascii"
             :step :prove
             :product #{seon.render.block-test/surface-id-escapes-every-character-outside-ascii-letters-digits-dot-and-hyphen
                        seon.render.block-test/every-surface-id-holds-only-ascii-letters-digits-dot-underscore-and-hyphen}
             :instructions "In `seon.render.block-test`, beside `seon.render.block-test/stable-surface-addresses-are-injective`, add two deftests, the step's declared product. (1) A table named surface-id-escapes-every-character-outside-ascii-letters-digits-dot-and-hyphen, `[name expected]` cases asserted with `clojure.core/=` on `(seon.render.block/surface-id name)`: [:transcript \"surface-transcript\"], [:a.b-c \"surface-a.b-c\"], [:a_b \"surface-a__b\"], [:my.turn/tree \"surface-my.turn_2f_tree\"], [(keyword \"a b\") \"surface-a_20_b\"], and the red cases [(keyword \"\\u00e9\") \"surface-_e9_\"], [(keyword \"\\u00aa\") \"surface-_aa_\"], [(keyword \"\\u0663\") \"surface-_663_\"] (build these three with `clojure.core/keyword` over a \\u escape, never a literal non-ASCII keyword). (2) A property named every-surface-id-holds-only-ascii-letters-digits-dot-underscore-and-hyphen, written like the existing one with `clojure.test.check/quick-check` 300 and a fixed `:seed` (use 20261013) under `seon.test-support/assert-check!`: for every name `(clojure.core/keyword s)` with s from `(clojure.test.check.generators/such-that seq clojure.test.check.generators/string)`, every character of `(subs (seon.render.block/surface-id name) 8)` is an ASCII letter, an ASCII digit, `.`, `_` or `-` (a small local predicate comparing `clojure.core/int` of the character against the ASCII ranges; no regex). Tag both `^{:seon/tasks #{'seon.render.block/implement-a-surface-id-passes-only-ascii}}`. Run them red on your branch: the three non-ASCII rows and the property (seed 20261013 shrinks to (keyword \"\\u00aa\")) are red; the other rows and the existing injectivity test are green."}
 #:seon.task{:name "implement-a-surface-id-passes-only-ascii"
             :needs #{[:seon.task/sym seon.render.block/prove-a-surface-id-passes-only-ascii]}
             :instructions "Make the table and the property green by changing `seon.render.block/surface-id` only: replace the Character/isLetterOrDigit test with an ASCII test (a-z, A-Z, 0-9), keeping `.` and `-` passing through, `_` doubling and every other character escaped as `_<hex>_`, so names that are already ASCII answer exactly what they answer today and `seon.render.block-test/stable-surface-addresses-are-injective` stays green. Keep its docstring and its contract `[:=> [:cat :seon.render.block/name] :seon.render/surface-id]` as they are, and correct the comment inside the body if it no longer says what the body does."}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-empty-block-name-answers-an-id-its-contract-refuses"
             :intent "`(seon.render.block/surface-id (keyword \"\"))` refuses its own output: the name is a valid `:seon.render.block/name` (a keyword), the body answers \"surface-\" (8 characters) and the output schema `:seon.render/surface-id` is a string of at least 9, so the instrumented call throws `:seon.instrument/contract-error` at its output check (observed in the JVM on default 3b5585017 under `clojure.test.check/quick-check` of `clojure.test.check.generators/string` names, which shrank to `:`). Decide at the owner which promise is wrong: a block name with an empty name is not a block name (tighten `:seon.render.block/name`), or the id of an empty name is \"surface-\" (loosen `:seon.render/surface-id` and say why). Read `seon.render.block/surface-id`."}
